{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/850793-osites-memento-mori-clarification-update","id":850793,"title":"Osites' Memento Mori clarification/update","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-03-21T13:50:55-07:00","id":850793,"title":"Osites' Memento Mori clarification/update","content":"I am trying to decipher the 3rd power called Brush of Death (Lancea Sanctum Covenant book, pg 180).<br /> <br /> Does the vampire need to make a called shot to a non-covered area (Arm, Head) in order to deal aggravated damage to another person, or does it work like a normal +0 aggravated unarmed attack ie, if there is any damage left after the armor deduction, it is applied as aggravated?<br /> <br /> I am confused by the use of the term &quot;reach the flesh&quot; abd the &quot;Touching the Opponent&quot; reference.<br /> <br /> Thank you in advance,<br /> P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>kill your god, kill your tv","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2261-urbanfractal","name":"urbanfractal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2261_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-21T19:09:08-07:00","id":850920,"title":"","content":"The Lancea Sanctum book was from 1e, so, the rules won't translate perfectly. It's my recommendation that it no longer deal aggravated, that it just become a 0L weapon, as the number of sources of aggravated damage have been severely reduced in 2e, and something that can deal lethal to vampires is still extremely valuable.<br /> <br /> The reference you don't understand is from the original world of darkness corebook. In 1e, you rolled Dex + Brawl - Defense to touch someone. If you didn't need to touch flesh, armor had no effect. If you did, as in this power, armor applied (Armor in 1e reduced your attack pool, rather than reducing damage after the roll, just as weapons added dice to your roll, rather than adding damage after).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-22T07:42:34-07:00","id":851129,"title":"","content":"I read the entry for &quot;Touching an Opponent&quot; in Second Edition Requiem pg 177, and it's the same as in the 1e WoD book. So what would be the attack roll, in your opinion, while using Brush of Death?<br /> <br /> a) Str + Brawl - Defense and then further reduce &lt;Armor&gt; points of damage (making this aspect of the power useful mostly in risqu\u00e9 situations)? If this is the case, plain clothes that do not offer any Armor rating, do they stop this power?<br /> or <br /> b) Dex + Brawl with no damage whatsoever, as per Touching an Opponent rules (making this power quite meaningless?<br /> <br /> Thanks","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>kill your god, kill your tv","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2261-urbanfractal","name":"urbanfractal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2261_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-22T08:28:30-07:00","id":851152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>urbanfractal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/850793-osites-memento-mori-clarification-update?p=851129#post851129\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I read the entry for &quot;Touching an Opponent&quot; in Second Edition Requiem pg 177, and it's the same as in the 1e WoD book. So what would be the attack roll, in your opinion, while using Brush of Death?</div> </div> </div> </div>If I had to convert it as plainly and literally as possible? I would say Dexterity + Brawl - Defense as if using a 0L weapon, including that armor reduces the damage.<br /> <br /> But I wouldn't convert the Ossites to 2e in the same way. I wouldn't make Memento Mori its own discipline, I'd give them Auspex, Celerity, Obfuscate, and Resilience and I'd probably reduce the severity of their flaw a little...double healing <i>or </i>they have to spend blood to prime themselves each scene, not both. <br /> <br /> I'd make seeing ghost a function of Auspex, or an Auspex devotion, just like the new khaibit. I'd make consult with the dead an Auspex devotion, too. Personally, I wouldn't convert Brush with Death because it feels like pointless filler, just an excuse to give them some aggravated damage, which was all over the place in 1e. If I HAD to convert it, I'd leave the damage part out and just keep the willpower draining aspect, making it an Auspex/Resilience devotion. Blood from Bone could definitely be an Auspex/Resilience devotion--it's perfect for that. Necrosis is another power I'd skip, but if I had to, it still fits as Auspex/Resilience. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-22T08:33:41-07:00","id":851159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/850793-osites-memento-mori-clarification-update?p=851152#post851152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I had to convert it as plainly and literally as possible? I would say Dexterity + Brawl - Defense as if using a 0L weapon, including that armor reduces the damage.<br /> <br /> But I wouldn't convert the Ossites to 2e in the same way. I wouldn't make Memento Mori its own discipline, I'd give them Auspex, Celerity, Obfuscate, and Resilience and I'd probably reduce the severity of their flaw a little...double healing <i>or </i>they have to spend blood to prime themselves each scene, not both.<br /> <br /> I'd make seeing ghost a function of Auspex, or an Auspex devotion, just like the new khaibit. I'd make consult with the dead an Auspex devotion, too. Personally, I wouldn't convert Brush with Death because it feels like pointless filler, just an excuse to give them some aggravated damage, which was all over the place in 1e. If I HAD to convert it, I'd leave the damage part out and just keep the willpower draining aspect, making it an Auspex/Resilience devotion. Blood from Bone could definitely be an Auspex/Resilience devotion--it's perfect for that. Necrosis is another power I'd skip, but if I had to, it still fits as Auspex/Resilience.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. &quot;Brush of Death&quot; should cause a Condition instead, like Spooked.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-22T09:14:22-07:00","id":851176,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the insight and the recommendations.<br /> <br /> On a relevant note, do you have any indication that the former Bloodline Disciplines will be transformed to devotions of the main Disciplines, in 2e?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>kill your god, kill your tv","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2261-urbanfractal","name":"urbanfractal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2261_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-22T09:44:25-07:00","id":851194,"title":"","content":"There are 3 official 2e bloodlines. The Khaibit lost obtenebration and it was replaced by a series of devotions. The Kerberos have a merit that gives them their special abilities. The Neglatu have an ability just flat out granted to them that doesn't fall into the discipline, devotion, or merit rules in any way.<br /> <br /> I believe it is far more likely for unusual bloodline abilities to take one of those three forms than to create an entirely new discipline. Plus, personally, I already felt like the unique disciplines were out of control and mostly pointless back in 1e. Most of the time, they're trying to force 2 or 3 great ideas into a 5 point spread, so, you get a single concept split across two dots for no reason, or you get unusual filler powers thrown in. You know, like, in a discipline devoted to learning about stuff after death (seeing ghosts, asking corpses questions, eating bones and learning skills the bone's owners had), you randomly get an aggravated death touch and a death ray tossed in.<br /> <br /> After the Carthian book, when devotions came into the limelight, I started converting almost all of the bloodlines with unique discipline into bloodlines with a normal set of disciplines and some unique devotions instead. Using a merit hadn't occured to me, but it is a great idea for a few. Regardless, it appears to me at least that the writers of 2e are following that path.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-23T00:12:08-07:00","id":851609,"title":"","content":"Are these Bloodline Devotions such a well kept secret as the Bloodline Disciplines in 1e? I am guessing they are, or have extra cost like learning an out-of-clan Discipline, apart from finding a willing member of another bloodline teach it to you. Or an &quot;outsider&quot; cannot learn them ever, but I am not sure that's what's in plan for Bloodlines in 2e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>kill your god, kill your tv","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2261-urbanfractal","name":"urbanfractal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2261_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-23T06:14:36-07:00","id":851696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>urbanfractal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/850793-osites-memento-mori-clarification-update?p=851609#post851609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are these Bloodline Devotions such a well kept secret as the Bloodline Disciplines in 1e? I am guessing they are, or have extra cost like learning an out-of-clan Discipline, apart from finding a willing member of another bloodline teach it to you. Or an &quot;outsider&quot; cannot learn them ever, but I am not sure that's what's in plan for Bloodlines in 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see why it needs to be be defined one way or the other. Run it how you like. In my games, no, you can't learn bloodline devotions without being from that bloodline. But if you prefer to run it another way, go for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}